+#### 3.2.20 Mutable-variable diagnostics
+
+Family:
+
+```text
+kappa.var.mutable-binding
+```
+
+Used when a source occurrence involving a surface `var` mutable binding is rejected or downgraded because the occurrence
+selects the wrong mutable-binding facet, attempts an effectful current-value read where no such read is permitted, or
+would violate quantity/linearity by duplicating or discarding the stored value.
+
+Diagnostics in this family include at least:
+
+```text
+E_VAR_REF_REQUIRED
+E_VAR_READ_OUTSIDE_DO
+E_VAR_READ_CROSSES_BOUNDARY
+E_VAR_IN_STATIC_POSITION
+E_VAR_READ_DUPLICATES_LINEAR_VALUE
+W_VAR_REF_ESCAPE_DISABLES_FLOW
+```
+
+Payload MUST include, when applicable:
+
+* the mutable binding name;
+* the selected facet, among `current-value`, `reference`, `assignment-target`, and `var-place`;
+* the type of the hidden reference cell;
+* the stored value type;
+* the enclosing `do` block and selected `MonadRef` evidence;
+* whether the occurrence was user-written or compiler-generated;
+* the expression-lifting boundary that blocked the read;
+* the source operation that required a `Ref`, when `ref x` is the local repair;
+* the quantity or path-state fact that made the stored value non-duplicable or non-droppable;
+* whether `ref x` escaped and which flow facts were invalidated; and
+* a source-preserving repair when evident.
+
+A diagnostic in this family SHOULD prefer local repairs such as writing `ref x`, binding a snapshot with `let x0 = x`,
+placing a read inside an explicit `do`, or replacing a current-value read with an explicit low-level `readRef (ref x)`
+when that is the intended operation.
+
+A diagnostic in this family MUST NOT suggest using `!x` as mutable-variable read syntax. The `!` form remains monadic
+splice syntax under §18.3.
+
